  "question": "Should I use ECS or a traditional object hierarchy?",
  "answer": "The excerpts don't directly compare ECS to traditional object hierarchies as architectural choices, so I can't give you a definitive answer on which to use.\n\nHowever, they do provide relevant context:\n\n**ECS's claimed advantage:** [2] argues the primary benefit is \"organization\" — composition lets you \"define sensibly repeatable structures\" and build new game objects by combining existing components without writing new logic. [4] adds that ECS \"helps make your code faster by optimizing memory access patterns and making parallelism easier.\"\n\n**But with caveats:** [2] emphasizes that high-performance ECS results depend on specific conditions (archetypal storage patterns) that \"the needs of most real-world game designs do not conform to.\" [3] counters that composition itself isn't unique to ECS — you can achieve it with simpler designs like flags and structs.\n\n**Practical note:** [1] shows that if you *do* use ECS, you can store hierarchies via parent/child components, though this has tradeoffs: component lookups can be slow for low-level systems, or you need frequent sorting for fast iteration.\n\nThe excerpts suggest ECS excels at *organizational clarity*, but warn against adopting it purely for performance benchmarks. Whether it beats a traditional hierarchy depends on your specific game's needs — something these excerpts don't address.",
